  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="1586-dbe5-3b2e-7590" name="Mob Rule!" hidden="false">
      <description>Ork mobs may always choose to substitute the number of Orks in their mob for their normal Leadership value. If an Ork mob numbers 11 or more models, it has the Fearless special rule.
Orks falling back can attempt to regroup as normal, but may also instead fall back towards any Ork mob that lies behind their position. If any Orks in the withdrawing unit get within 6&quot; of the new mob once moves are complete then the withdrawing mob attempts to join with the new one. Roll 2D6 again the Leadership value of the falling back mob. If successful the two mobs combing together into one unit. The previously withdrawing mob can immediately move up to 6&quot; to be in coherency with the second unit.
Only Ork foot units will mob up in this manner. Slavers and Meks in Grot mobs or Big Gunz, Stormboyz and Warbikes may not mob up.
A mob of Orks that mobs up with another unit counts as destroyed for victory points purposes. </description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="7bb4-34ca-51f3-bb8c" name="WAAAGH!" hidden="false">
      <description>Once per game, the Ork player can declare a Waaagh! at the start of his Shooting phase. This may not normally be declared on the first turn as a proper Waaagh! needs some momentum behind it. For the duration of that turn, all friendly Ork infantry units have the Fleet of Foot rule (not Gretchin units, they&apos;re far too weedy for a proper Waaagh!).</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="496b-e8d5-33fe-9edb" name="Gretchin Mobs" hidden="false">
      <description>Gretchin who fail a Moral check caused by enemy shooting or tank shock or which fail to regroup after falling back from close combat the grots &quot;go to ground.&quot; Remove all of the gretchin models from the board. If no slaver is present the mob is destroyed. If a slaver is present they remain on the board and attempts to regroup each turn, even if the Gretchin mob had been reduced below 50% starting strength or the enemy are within 6&quot; 
The slaver trying to regroup the Gretchin mob cannot move, shoot or initiate an assault, but can be shot at or charged. They fight back in close combat as normal. If the slaver is killed the Gretchin unit is now destroyed. </description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="249e-3d2c-b96b-7edc" name="Glory Hogs" hidden="false">
      <description>Tankbustas must always attempt to shoot at and/or assault an enemy vehicle if there is one in line of sight, regardless of range. If there is no visible vehicle target, the Tankbustas may select a target as normal.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="cd1b-12a9-0ceb-301d" name="Living Shield" hidden="false">
      <description>Any Ork mobs which are shot at &quot;through&quot; a Grot mob can claim a 5+ cover save. Each successful save causes a Grot to be removed as a casualty.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="39ee-155c-f8e8-f5c1" name="Better Footing" hidden="false">
      <description>If a Grot mob is in difficult ground any Ork mobs moving through the same difficult ground my re-roll the dice for how far they move. Mega Armored Orks may not use this ability.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="c948-4917-842b-6b56" name="Mine Clearance" hidden="false">
      <description>If a Grot mob moves into a minefield remove 3D6 Grots as casualties and then remove the minefield marker. If there are fewer Grots remaining in the mob than the rolled dice than the Grot mob is destroyed and the minefield is not removed.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="d2e0-6a69-f681-96f4" name="Exhaust Cloud" hidden="false">
      <description>Due to dust and exhaust clouds Warbikes give their riders a 4+ cover saving throw. Furthermore a unit being shot at &quot;through&quot; the warbikes also recieve the 5+ cover saving throw.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="8921-5e15-774b-8939" name="Hits on the Battery" hidden="false">
      <description>When a battary of Big Gunz is fired at, randomize any hits between the krew and the gunz. Roll a D6:
1-4: Hits the Krew
5-6: Hits a Big Gun
Hits against the Gunz is treated like a vehicle with an armor value of 10, but any glancing or penetrating hits destroy them.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="173b-0e54-5eea-964e" name="Kustom Job: More Dakka" hidden="false">
      <description>A weapon with the More Dakka kustom job has different effect based on weapon type: A Pistol becomes Assault 2, an Assault weapon increases shots by 1 (I.e. Assault 2 becomes Assault 3) and a Heavy X weapon may re-roll the amount of shots generated.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="bc5b-2941-29a7-ce0b" name="Kustom Job: Shootier" hidden="false">
      <description>A weapon with the Shootier kustom job increases its strength value by 1.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="9907-7c8b-718f-0156" name="Kustom Job: Blasta" hidden="false">
      <description>A weapon with the Blasta kustom job gains the Get Hot! rule and increases its AP value to AP3 at targets up to 12&quot; and AP2 at targets up to 6&quot;.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="e312-e1c4-31fb-733d" name="&apos;Eavy Armour" hidden="false">
      <description>An Ork wearing &apos;Eavy Armour has a 4+ Armor Save.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="4a24-7d6e-a253-5ace" name="Grot Bombs" hidden="false">
      <description>Treated as a Smart Bomb. You may re-roll the scatter dice if you don&apos;t like the first result, but you must accept the second result.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="b365-be2c-7c66-565c" name="Runtbot" hidden="false">
      <description>A Runtbot is treated as a mobile Gun Emplacement without any weapons. Instead each time the unit it is with is shot at, roll a D6. If the D6 is equal to or less than the current Turn number the Runtbots shield flickers and fails for the remainder of the Turn. Otherwise reduce the Strength of the incoming shots by the result of the D6 roll. For example on Turn 2, if the dice roll is a 1 or a 2, the Shield fails, but if it is a 3, 4, 5 or 6, reduce the Strength of the incoming shots by that amount.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="95cc-6a27-8c58-0755" name="Release the Squigs!" hidden="false">
      <description>At the start of your Movement or Assault phase the Runtherd may choose to release his Squig Pack. Nominate an enemy unit as the target for the Squig pack; The Squig pack then immediately splits off from the Runtherd and is considered a seperate unit from this point forward. The Squig pack must move towards its nominated target as quickly as possible and assault if possible. If the nominated target is destroyed or otherwise removed from the table, the Squig pack will target the next closest enemy infantry unit on a dice roll of 2+, on a roll of 1 they will begin either infighting, wandering off or otherwise losing coherency and are thus promply removed as casualties.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="644a-6ea9-a6c4-3558" name="Weirdboy Psychic Powers" hidden="false">
      <description>Weirdboys cannot control their psychic powers. At the beginning of each Ork Shooting phase, a Weirdboy must roll on the following table to findout which psychic power he must use that turn.

Weirdboys must take a Psychic test before determining the power they use. If the test is failed, do not roll to determine which power he uses. Remember that a Weirdboy&apos;s Leadership is affected by Mob Rule. A Weirdboy using the Frazzle or Zzap power is coutned as having shot a weapon that phase. If the Weirdboy is in combat, do not apply any results of a 1, 2, or 3 - instead the Weirdboy is treated as having a power weapon for the duration of that turn.

D6 Result
1 - &apos;Eadbanger: Resolve the Frazzle psychic power detailed below with the template centred on the Weirdboy himself.
2 - Frazzle: Choose an enemy unit within line of sight. If in range, that unit is automatically hit. Frazzle has the related weapon profile.
3 - Zzap: Choose an enemy unit within line of sight. If in range, that unit is automatically hit. Zzap has the related weapon profile.
4 - Warpath: All Orks in the Weirdboy&apos;s unit have +1A until the beginning of the next Ork turn.
5 - &apos;Ere We Go: The Weirdboy and any unit he is with must be placed anywhere on the board as per the Deep Strike rules. This power must be used even if enemy models are in base contact; if so the enemy models stay in place.
6 - Waaagh!: A Waaagh! is automatically invoked this turn. This does not use up the normal Waaagh! available to the Ork player. Such is the power of the Weirdboy&apos;s energy discharge that this may even allow the Orks to use a Waaagh! on the first turn of the game. Note that multiple Waaaghs! have no cumulative effect.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="ab6a-05a0-4e17-a576" name="Warphead" hidden="false">
      <description>Each turn, a Weirdboy that has been upgraded to a Warphead may re-roll the dice to see which psychic power he uses.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="6d7a-5632-5fc8-cf60" name="Stikkbomb Chukka" hidden="false">
      <description>A stikkbomb chukka comes equipped with Frag Stikkbombz and allows a mega armoured Ork to use them.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="3dd7-36a6-3689-2e91" name="Dok&apos;s Tools" hidden="false">
      <description>The Painboy or Painboss confers the Feel No Pain (6+) special rule to his unit.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="7830-7835-edfe-d584" name="Mek&apos;s Tools" hidden="false">
      <description>If a Mekboy or Big Mek is inside (or in base contact with) a vehicle at the beginning of his Shooting phase, he can attempt to repair a single Weapon Destroyed or Immobilised (or Vehicle Destroyed in the case of a Big Gun) result instead of shooting. Roll a D6 - on a roll of a 4+ the result is negated. This could allow a previously destroyed weapon to fire in that Shooting phase. On a roll of a 1, the Mek gets a bit carried away and the vehicle counts as shaken.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="8205-3ee0-e915-f850" name="Cybork Body" hidden="false">
      <description>An Ork with a Cybork Body has a 5+ Invulnerable Saving Throw.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="e842-2215-0e94-0c13" name="Kombi-Weapons" hidden="false">
      <description>A model armed with a kombi-weapon may choose which of the weapons it is going to use in the Shooting phase. Ork Kombi-weapons are more crude and unreliable than their imperium combi-weapons counterparts, and thus while the shoota may be fired any number of time, but the other weapon may only be fired once per battle. Note that you may not choose to fire both weapons at once.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="a3e2-7319-56b8-e161" name="Bomb-squigs" hidden="false">
      <description>A model may release a Bomb-squig instead of shooting. The Bomb-squig then immediately splits off from the unit and is considered a seperate unit from this point forward. Nominate an enemy vehicle as the target for the Bomb-squig and roll a dice. On a roll of 2+ the Bomb-squig must move the shortest distance towards its nominated target each turn and assault if possible, causing a single S8 hit on the facing it charged. On a roll of 1 the nearest friendly vehicle must instead be nominated as the target, and be treated as an enemy model for the bomb-squig moving and assaulting as above.
Bomb-squig models cannot be charged or otherwise fight in close combat, and may move through enemy models (It may still not end its movement within 1&quot; of an enemy model) as well as friendly models to get to their target. (Units will simple step aside when it comes barreling, nobody wants to take a fistfull of dynamite to the face!)</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="7b34-2c6f-1efd-b1aa" name="Squig Pack" hidden="false">
      <description>When a Suig Pack is formed, nominate an enemy unit as the target for the Squig pack. The Squig pack must move towards its nominated target as quickly as possible and assault if possible. If the nominated target is destroyed or otherwise removed from the table, the Squig pack will target the next closest enemy infantry unit on a dice roll of 2+, on a roll of 1 they will begin either infighting, wandering off or otherwise losing coherency and are thus promply removed as casualties.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="625e-1e19-52c5-4b67" name="Big Horns" hidden="false">
      <description>Orks with Big Horns gain +2 Attacks when charging instead of +1.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="b3f4-6124-59db-84b1" name="Ammo Runt" hidden="false">
      <description>A model with an ammo runt is allowed to re-roll one to hit roll for a shooting attack. The ammo runt is removed once the re-roll has been used.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="05e1-26b8-8e88-383b" name="Gitfinda" hidden="false">
      <description>When shooting, An Ork with a Gitfinda may measure to see if they are in range before declaring their target.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="a17c-172e-e3cf-c4d9" name="Rokkit Pack" hidden="false">
      <description>Rokkit packs count as jump packs. Every time a unit utilises its rokkit packs to move or fall back, roll a D6. On a roll of 1, one of the rokkit packs has gone out of control and explodes. Remove a model with Rokkit Pack from the unit as a casualty. Regardless of the result you may add the number rolls to the amount the unit moves that turn.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="43e1-aa30-b793-1d69" name="Reslience" hidden="false">
      <description>A Tyrant is incredibly reslient, able to shrug off most small arms fire and even survive blows from heavy weapons that would obliterate lesser orks. A Tyrant is immune to instant death, (taking a single wound instead) and has the Feel no Pain (4+) universal special rule.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="3d81-c1b7-a99d-6d10" name="Shoot the Big One!" hidden="false">
      <description>Unless joined by a squad of Tyrant&apos;s Guard&apos;s, a Tyrant may always be the target of shooting attacks, even if joined with other units.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="6a30-1d46-0574-2cdd" name="Grot Gunner" hidden="false">
      <description>A weapon with a Grot Gunner shoots with a base BS value of 4 instead of the models BS. (i.e. hitting on 4+ instead of 5+.)</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="1678-0113-a4f0-95b0" name="Moar Dakka!" hidden="false">
      <description>A model with this rule may fire all equipped weapons each shooting face instead of of only shooting a single.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="02b7-a40c-b880-081a" name="Big &apos;ands" hidden="false">
      <description>A model with Big &apos;ands counts two handed weapons as one handed and may thus gain an additional attack from having two close combat weapons.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="752b-c990-1d9e-bdc4" name="Kustom Force Field" hidden="false">
      <description>A kustom force field gives all units within 6&quot; of the Mek a cover save of 5+. Vehicles with 50% of their model within 6&quot; are treated as being obscured targets. The force field has no effect in an assault. If the Mek is inside a transport, measure from the centre of the transport.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="810d-68ab-fd52-6cf1" name="Armour Plates" hidden="false">
      <description>A vehicle with armour plates treats &apos;Crew Stunned&apos; results as &apos;Crew Shaken&apos; results instead.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="9034-f624-1935-f123" name="Ramshackle Monster" hidden="false">
      <description>This walker is a heavily armoured, smoke-belching, monstrosity that is difficult to stop once it&apos;s gotten going! Each time it would suffer a result on the vehicle damage table, roll a D6. On a roll of 5+ ignore the damage result.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule id="a533-b56b-e4b0-15d8" name="Mega-charga" hidden="false">
      <description>This upgrade may be used once per game. At the start of the Movement phase, roll a D6. On the roll of a 1 the walker is immobilissed (note the Ramshackle Monster rule do not apply to this damage). On any other result, the walker gains the Fleet universal special rule for this turn.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
